{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01BgoxpqasZ38QFLTpNqEgtf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Translation contract (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n2\t\n3\tWe are translating **libsodium 1.0.23** from `c_src/libsodium/**` into a Rust\n4\t`cdylib` living in `src/`. The Rust `.so` must export the *exact same* symbol\n5\tnames as the C `.so` and behave byte-identically.\n6\t\n7\t## Hard rules\n8\t\n9\t1. **Never modify anything under `c_src/`.** It is the reference.\n10\t2. **One C file → one Rust file.** The Rust file name is the C basename with\n11\t   `-` replaced by `_` and extension `.rs`, placed directly in `src/`.\n12\t   e.g. `crypto_generichash/blake2b/ref/blake2b-ref.c` → `src/blake2b_ref.rs`.\n13\t   The module is already declared in `src/lib.rs`; do not touch `lib.rs`.\n14\t3. **Only translate the preprocessor path the reference build takes.**\n15\t   The CMake build defines **no** `HAVE_*` macros, no `NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN`,\n16\t   no `NATIVE_BIG_ENDIAN`, no `HAVE_TI_MODE`, no `__SSE2__`/`__AVX2__`/etc.,\n17\t   no `HAVE_INLINE_ASM`, no `HAVE_LIBCTGRIND`, no `SODIUM_LIBRARY_MINIMAL`,\n18\t   no `_MSC_VER`, no `__native_client__`, no `__EMSCRIPTEN__`, no `__wasi__`.\n19\t   It **is** Linux/glibc x86-64 ELF, `__GNUC__` defined, `__ELF__` defined,\n20\t   `HAVE_MMAP`/`HAVE_MPROTECT`/`HAVE_MLOCK`/`HAVE_MADVISE`/`HAVE_POSIX_MEMALIGN`\n21\t   are **NOT** defined (configure was not run — no `config.h`), so take the\n22\t   fallback branches for those too. When in doubt, run the C preprocessor\n23\t   yourself to see what survives:\n24\t   ```\n25\t   gcc -E -Ic_src/libsodium/include -Ic_src/libsodium/include/sodium \\\n26\t       -Ic_src/libsodium/<subsystem-dir> <file.c> | less\n27\t   ```\n28\t4. **Exported functions.** Every C function with *external* linkage (i.e. not\n29\t   `static`) becomes:\n30\t   ```rust\n31\t   #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n32\t   pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn <FINAL_LINKER_NAME>(...) -> ... { ... }\n33\t   ```\n34\t   The `<FINAL_LINKER_NAME>` is the name **after** macro renaming performed by\n35\t   `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/private/quirks.h`. That header renames e.g.\n36\t   `ge25519_p3_tobytes` → `_sodium_ge25519_p3_tobytes`,\n37\t   `blake2b_update` → `_sodium_blake2b_update`,\n38\t   `softaes_block_encrypt` → `_sodium_softaes_block_encrypt`, etc.\n39\t   **Always check quirks.h** for every non-`static` function you translate.\n40\t   The authoritative per-file symbol list is in `SYMBOLS.md`; your file's\n41\t   exported names MUST match it exactly (no more, no fewer).\n42\t5. **`static` C functions / variables** become plain private Rust items. Never\n43\t   put `#[no_mangle]` on them.\n44\t6. **Cross-file calls.** If your file calls a function defined in a *different*\n45\t   C file, declare it locally:\n46\t   ```rust\n47\t   extern \"C\" {\n48\t       fn crypto_hash_sha512(out: *mut u8, in_: *const u8, inlen: u64) -> c_int;\n49\t   }\n50\t   ```\n51\t   using the FINAL linker name. Do **not** `use crate::other_module::...` for\n52\t   these — the linker resolves them inside the cdylib. This keeps files\n53\t   independent. (Exception: `crate::common` helpers, see below.)\n54\t7. **Exported global variables** (the `*_implementation` structs):\n55\t   ```rust\n56\t   #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n57\t   pub static crypto_stream_chacha20_ref_implementation:\n58\t       crypto_stream_chacha20_implementation = ...;\n59\t   ```\n60\t   All structs shared across the FFI boundary must be `#[repr(C)]`, with\n61\t   **exact** field order/sizes/alignment from the C header.\n62\t8. **No behaviour changes.** Reproduce integer overflow/wrapping, evaluation\n63\t   order, error-check order, and even apparent bugs exactly. Use\n64\t   `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_mul`/`<<`-with-mask etc. as needed; the crate is\n65\t   built with `overflow-checks = false` but be explicit anyway.\n66\t9. `libc` is **not** a dependency. Use `std` (the crate links std). For\n67\t   syscalls not in std, declare them yourself in an `extern \"C\"` block\n68\t   (`extern \"C\" { fn getrandom(buf: *mut c_void, len: usize, flags: c_uint) -> isize; }`\n69\t   etc.).\n70\t10. Do not add `#[cfg(target_arch)]` gates. Target is x86-64 Linux.\n71\t11. `sodium_runtime_has_*()` all return 0 in the reference build (no HAVE_*),\n72\t    so any `if (sodium_runtime_has_x())` dispatch resolves to the fallback.\n73\t    Still translate the dispatch code faithfully (call the extern fn).\n74\t\n75\t## Available shared helpers — `crate::common`\n76\t\n77\t`src/common.rs` already provides (all `pub`):\n78\t\n79\t```rust\n80\tpub fn rotl32(x: u32, b: i32) -> u32;\n81\tpub fn rotl64(x: u64, b: i32) -> u64;\n82\tpub fn rotr32(x: u32, b: i32) -> u32;\n83\tpub fn rotr64(x: u64, b: i32) -> u64;\n84\tpub unsafe fn load64_le(src: *const u8) -> u64;\n85\tpub unsafe fn store64_le(dst: *mut u8, w: u64);\n86\tpub unsafe fn load32_le(src: *const u8) -> u32;\n87\tpub unsafe fn store32_le(dst: *mut u8, w: u32);\n88\tpub unsafe fn load64_be(src: *const u8) -> u64;\n89\tpub unsafe fn store64_be(dst: *mut u8, w: u64);\n90\tpub unsafe fn load32_be(src: *const u8) -> u32;\n91\tpub unsafe fn store32_be(dst: *mut u8, w: u32);\n92\tpub unsafe fn xor_buf(out: *mut u8, in_: *const u8, n: usize);\n93\tpub const SODIUM_SIZE_MAX: u64;\n94\tpub unsafe fn memcpy(dst: *mut u8, src: *const u8, n: usize);\n95\tpub unsafe fn memmove(dst: *mut u8, src: *const u8, n: usize);\n96\tpub unsafe fn memset(dst: *mut u8, c: u8, n: usize);\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tUse `use crate::common::*;` at the top of your file.\n100\t\n101\t## Style notes\n102\t\n103\t* Start each file with:\n104\t  ```rust\n105\t  use crate::common::*;\n106\t  use core::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_uint, c_ulonglong, c_void};\n107\t  ```\n108\t  (import only what you need).\n109\t* Prefer raw-pointer arithmetic that mirrors the C, or\n110\t  `core::slice::from_raw_parts{,_mut}` where lengths are known. Correctness and\n111\t  fidelity beat elegance.\n112\t* `unsigned long long` → `c_ulonglong` (u64). `size_t` → `usize`.\n113\t  `unsigned char *` → `*mut u8`. `const unsigned char *` → `*const u8`.\n114\t  `char *` → `*mut c_char`. `int` → `c_int`.\n115\t* For `static` mutable C globals use `static mut` + `unsafe`, or\n116\t  `UnsafeCell`/atomics if that is more convenient — but the observable\n117\t  behaviour must match.\n118\t* When a C function is `static inline` in a header shared by several .c files,\n119\t  just duplicate it as a private helper in each Rust file that needs it.\n120\t\n121\t## Verification\n122\t\n123\tAfter writing, run from the crate root:\n124\t```\n125\tcargo build --release 2>&1 | tail -40\n126\t```\n127\tand fix all errors. Then check your symbols:\n128\t```\n129\tnm -D --defined-only target/release/libsodium.so | awk '{print $3}' | sort\n130\t```\n131\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GzrQcVNDzFSMp1SSDmmPxy","session_id":"dbed778e-b40b-44c3-aae8-c706753ef0ef","uuid":"13658906-7c2b-4021-8af1-816b71ea294b","timestamp":"2026-08-22T13:37:06.912Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate sodium/utils.c and version.c"}
