n 16, with `offset` @224, `phase` @232, `domain` @233. Modeled as `#[repr(C, align(16))]` + `#[repr(C)]`.\n- **Rates / rounds**: shake128 & turboshake128 rate 168; shake256 & turboshake256 rate 136. The shake variants use `crypto_core_keccak1600_permute_24`, the turboshake variants use `permute_12` (including the finalize permutation). Domain standard is `0x1F` for all four.\n- **Keccak calls** are declared in local `extern \"C\"` blocks using the *unrenamed* public names `crypto_core_keccak1600_{init,xor_bytes,extract_bytes,permute_24,permute_12}` — `quirks.h` only renames the `keccak1600_ref_*` internals, which these files never call directly.\n- The one-shot `*_ref()` functions use `MaybeUninit` for the on-stack state (matching C's uninitialized local; `keccak1600_ref_init` only zeroes 200 of the 224 state bytes, and the remaining 24 are never read).\n- The `COMPILER_ASSERT(sizeof(state) >= sizeof(internal))` checks in the `xof_*.c` wrappers are reproduced as `const _: () = assert!(...)`.\n\n## Functional verification\n\nBuilt a C driver exercising all four XOFs across 16×16 input/output length combinations (0, 1, 2, 7, 135/136/137, 167/168/169, 200, 335/336/337, 504, 1000 — i.e. straddling both rates), covering one-shot, streamed absorb/squeeze in odd-sized chunks, the squeeze→update phase-flip path (checking the `-1` return), and `init_with_domain(0x07)`. Compiled it twice — once against the original C sources, once linked against the Rust `.so` plus the real C keccak1600 objects — and the 4100 lines of output are **identical** (`diff` exit 0). Spot-checked SHAKE128(\"\") = `7f9c2ba4...` against the NIST vector.\n\nNote in passing: my first driver revision had a bug (reading `in[0..5]` when `inlen < 5`, i.e. uninitialized stack) which produced 48 spurious differing lines; fixing the driver made the diff empty. Nothing was changed in the Rust to make that pass.\n\n`cargo build --release` on the full crate finishes with exit 0 and no errors or warnings referencin