e crate):\n```\n_sodium_sc25519_invert\n_sodium_sc25519_is_canonical\n_sodium_sc25519_mul\n_sodium_sc25519_muladd\n_sodium_sc25519_reduce\n```\ni.e. sc.rs owns ALL FIVE of these `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn`\ndefinitions; `_sodium_sc25519_mul` and `_sodium_sc25519_muladd` simply forward to\n`crate::ed25519_ref10::sc_mul::sc25519_mul` / `sc25519_muladd`.\nSignatures:\n```rust\n_sodium_sc25519_invert(recip: *mut u8, s: *const u8)\n_sodium_sc25519_reduce(s: *mut u8)\n_sodium_sc25519_mul(s: *mut u8, a: *const u8, b: *const u8)\n_sodium_sc25519_muladd(s: *mut u8, a: *const u8, b: *const u8, c: *const u8)\n_sodium_sc25519_is_canonical(s: *const u8) -> core::ffi::c_int\n```\nAlso expose plain `pub unsafe fn sc25519_reduce`, `sc25519_invert`,\n`sc25519_is_canonical` (no mangling) for sibling modules.\n\nFIDELITY REQUIREMENTS for `sc25519_reduce`: it is ~330 lines of unrolled statements like\n```c\n    s0  = 2097151 & load_3(s);\n    s1  = 2097151 & (load_4(s + 2) >> 5);\n    ...\n    s11 += s23 * 666643;\n    s12 += s23 * 470296;\n    ...\n    carry0 = (s0 + (int64_t) (1 << 20)) >> 21; s1 += carry0; s0 -= carry0 << 21;\n    ...\n    s[0] = (unsigned char) (s0 >> 0);\n```\nTRANSCRIBE EVERY LINE MECHANICALLY AND EXACTLY — no simplification, no loops, no\nreordering. Use a Python script to do the conversion (read the exact line range from the\nC source, apply regex rewrites), then hand-review a random sample of 40 lines and verify\nthe statement count matches the C (`grep -c ';'`).\nConversion rules:\n  `int64_t x;` decls        -> `let mut x: i64 = 0;`\n  `2097151 & load_3(s)`     -> `2097151 & load_3(s) as i64`\n  `load_4(s + 2) >> 5`      -> `load_4(s.add(2)) >> 5`\n  `a += b * 666643;`        -> `a = a.wrapping_add(b.wrapping_mul(666643));`\n  `a -= b * 683901;`        -> `a = a.wrapping_sub(b.wrapping_mul(683901));`\n  `carry0 << 21`            -> `carry0 << 21`   (i64 shift)\n  `carry0 * ((uint64_t) 1L << 21)` -> `carry0.wrapping_mul(1i64 << 21)`\n  `