{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zyznybpxoi2klzvnnozp7m6gjcslal3kvwvxiswpkwnngjh5s4sa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three files are complete and verified. Here's the summary.\n\n## Files translated\n\n**`src/randombytes.rs`** (from `randombytes/randombytes.c`) — 10 exported symbols:\n`randombytes`, `randombytes_buf`, `randombytes_buf_deterministic`, `randombytes_close`, `randombytes_implementation_name`, `randombytes_random`, `randombytes_seedbytes`, `randombytes_set_implementation`, `randombytes_stir`, `randombytes_uniform`\n\n**`src/randombytes_sysrandom.rs`** (from `randombytes/sysrandom/randombytes_sysrandom.c`) — 1 exported symbol:\n`randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` (`D`, 48 bytes)\n\n**`src/randombytes_internal_random.rs`** (from `randombytes/internal/randombytes_internal_random.c`) — 1 exported symbol:\n`randombytes_internal_implementation` (`D`, 48 bytes)\n\nExactly matches SYMBOLS.md — no more, no fewer. Both data symbols are 48 bytes (0x30) in `.data.rel.ro`, reported as `D` by `nm`, matching the C `.so`.\n\n## Two non-obvious findings about the reference build\n\nThe harness had **already built** the C reference at `$TMPDIR/cbuild` (cache dated before my session) with `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, giving `-O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIC -std=gnu99`. That changes two things versus a naive `gcc -E`:\n\n1. **`-DNDEBUG`** ⇒ every `assert()` collapses to `((void)(0))`. My first draft routed them through `__assert_fail`, which would have aborted where C silently continues (e.g. `randombytes_linux_getrandom(buf, 0)` must return 0, and `safe_read(fd, buf, 0)` must return 0). Fixed — asserts are now comments only.\n2. **`-std=gnu99`** ⇒ `__STDC_VERSION__ == 199901L`, so the `TLS` macro in `randombytes_internal_random.c` selects the `# define TLS` (empty) fallback. `stream` is a **plain process-wide static, not `_Thread_local`**. My first draft used `thread_local!`, which produced observably different values across threads. Fixed to `static mut`.\n\nOther confirmed preprocessor outcomes: `HAVE_LINUX_COMPATIBLE_GETRANDOM` **is** defined (via `syscall(318, …)` from `<sys/syscall.h>`, even though `HAVE_GETRANDOM` is not), `BLOCK_ON_DEV_RANDOM` **is** defined (so `/dev/random` is polled first), `S_ISNAM(x)` is `0`, `xorhwrand()` is empty, and `safe_read()` in the internal file is genuinely unreferenced (the getrandom branch of `stir()` wins, so the key is never read from the fd — faithfully reproduced).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo build --release`: **0 errors, 0 warnings**; the full crate now links (other agents' files finished too).\n- Built the C reference and ran a **differential test** (dlopen both `.so`s, identical harness, `LD_PRELOAD` interposing `syscall`/`gettimeofday`/`open`/`read`/`close` for determinism): 4 configurations × ~200 lines of output each — sysrandom + internal, single- and multi-threaded, getrandom path and `/dev/urandom`-fallback path (with short reads to exercise `safe_read`'s loop). **All byte-for-byte identical**, including `randombytes_close()` returning `-1` vs `0` per path.\n- `randombytes_buf_deterministic` output independently cross-checked against a from-scratch ChaCha20-IETF implementation: `0d8e6cc68715648926732e7ea73250cf`.\n\nOne note: `randombytes` and `randombytes_buf` share an address in the Rust `.so` (LLVM identical-code folding — the NaCl wrapper is identical once the tautological assert is gone). Both symbols are exported and behave identically, so the symbol check and behaviour are unaffected.\n\nCaveat worth flagging: I rebuilt `$TMPDIR/cbuild/libsodium.so` in place (it reused the existing cache, so identical flags). I verified its exported symbol list is identical to the harness's pre-existing `$TMPDIR/c_syms.txt`, but if the harness expected that file untouched, it has a fresh build-id."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2110